"""
Connect 4 positions solved exactly, written down so an optimisation cannot change an answer.

The regression gate for `ai.oracle`. Every entry here was produced by the plain full-width
negamax the solver was before alpha-beta, a transposition table, move ordering and mirror
sharing were added to it - each of which is a change that makes the solver faster and *could*
make it wrong, silently, in one position out of thousands. A solver that has become the oracle
everything downstream trusts cannot be graded against itself, so this file is its independent
memory of what it used to say.

Each entry is `(ply, moves, value, optimal)`:

    ply      how many discs are on the board, which is also `len(moves)`
    moves    the columns played from an empty board, in order
    value    the game-theoretic result to the player to move: 1 win, 0 draw, -1 loss
    optimal  every move that holds that value, sorted

**The optimal move set, not just the value.** A solver can get a position's value right while
disagreeing about which moves hold it, and the move set is what the benchmark actually grades
against - so it is the part that has to be pinned.

Forty positions at each even ply from 22 to 34, sampled by seeded random play. Stratified because
a regression at one stage of the game must not be able to hide behind another: the endgame
positions are trivial for any solver and would drown out a mid-game fault if the sample were
drawn uniformly. Plies below 22 are absent because they were unaffordable for the full-width
solver that generated this - which is the whole reason for the optimisation this file guards.
"""

